{"id":"int-33ff119c9ad0f1b59365b3a462cb5f07","kind":"field_change","created_at":"2026-08-09T22:21:19.254487Z","actor":"Edwin Steele","issue_id":"fps-luw","extra":{"field":"status","new_value":"closed","old_value":"in_progress","reason":"Design complete. Root cause confirmed by profiling against the real 492MB DB: per-snapshot re-query over overlapping trailing windows is 59% of classify.py and 99% of lga_leadership.py; scipy/trough detection is 1%.\n\nDecision: both range paths load once and slice, but with different shapes, because memory is the binding constraint on Viking:\n- lga_leadership (SQL already aggregates to (date,LGA); full history = 106k rows / 30 MB) -> single full-range _load_lga_sums + date-bucketed searchsorted slicing. Parity provable: HAVING groups on (price_date, council), which no window can split.\n- classify (raw per-station rows; full history = 2.23M rows / 540 MB resident, 881 MB peak -> would OOM Viking) -> one ORDER BY price_date scan feeding a 45-day deque of per-day buckets, ~7 MB resident. Yield buckets, not flattened rows.\nSingle-snapshot paths (the daily daily-db-update.yml path) unchanged in both.\n\nPrototyped both: windows byte-identical to current queries. Projected on a GHA runner: classify 471s -> ~90s, lga_leadership 1019s -> ~40s; the two steps 25min -> ~2min, seed-db.yml 46min -> ~23min (history.py's network-bound 16min becomes the bottleneck).\n\nConvention recorded in AGENTS.md § 'Backfill (--start-date) paths: load once, slice in memory'.\nImplementation split into fps-53j (lga_leadership, do first — bigger win, simpler) and fps-2us (classify)."}}
